{"type":"data","nodes":[null,{"type":"data","data":[{"mission":1,"hero":509},{"id":2,"agency":3,"agency_full":4,"sector":5,"dest":6,"color":7,"year":8,"status":9,"departure_date":10,"arrival_date":11,"transit_days":12,"round_trip":13,"vehicle":14,"payload":15,"delta_v":16,"data_quality":17,"credit":18,"links":19,"flight_data_quality":31,"fleet_refs":32,"flight":36,"name":484,"type":485,"first":486,"description":487,"events":488,"vehicle_stage":38},"mmx","JAXA","Japan Aerospace Exploration Agency","gov","MARS","#003087",2026,"ACTIVE","2026-10-19","2027-08-01",286,true,"H3-24L","4480 kg","~5 km/s","partial","© JAXA. Launched 2026-10-19 19:41 UTC on H3 flight 10 from Tanegashima (delayed from 2024 by H3 development); separation confirmed 71 min after liftoff. Mars orbit planned August 2027; the Phobos sample (≥10 g) is to reach Earth in 2031.",[20,24,27],{"l":21,"u":22,"t":23},"Martian Moons eXploration — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martian_Moons_Exploration","intro",{"l":25,"u":26,"t":23},"MMX (JAXA)","https://www.mmx.jaxa.jp/en/",{"l":28,"u":29,"t":30},"Campagnola et al. — MMX mission analysis (Acta Astronautica 2018)","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.actaastro.2018.03.024","deep","sparse",[33],{"id":34,"role":35},"h3","launcher",{"launch":37,"cruise":42,"arrival":45,"totals":51,"events":55,"interplanetary_profile":91},{"vehicle_stage":38,"c3_km2_s2":39,"mass_at_tli_kg":40,"source":41},"H3-24L second stage",12,4480,"JAXA launch result, H3 F10 (2026-10-20 JST); MMX press kit (Sept 2026) for the 4480 kg launch mass; C3 is the pre-launch target value",{"tcm_count":43,"source":44},4,"JAXA MMX trajectory plan (planned-mission target)",{"v_infinity_km_s":46,"periapsis_km":47,"inclination_deg":48,"orbit_insertion_dv_km_s":49,"source":50},2.6,6000,1,0.95,"Kawakatsu et al. (2019); MMX targets Phobos quasi-satellite orbit before descent",{"total_dv_km_s":52,"tli_or_tmi_dv_km_s":53,"source":54},5,3.7,"JAXA MMX project (planned, includes Phobos sample-grab + Earth return)",[56,61,66,71,75,78,82,86],{"met_days":57,"type":58,"label":59,"description":60},0,"launch","Launch — H3 flight 10","Tanegashima, Japan, 19 October 2026 at 19:41 UTC (20 October, 04:41 JST) — the first H3 to send a spacecraft to another planet. MMX (Martian Moons eXploration) is JAXA's sample-return mission to Phobos, the larger and inner of Mars's two moons, in the line of Hayabusa 1 and 2.",{"met_days":62,"type":63,"label":64,"description":65},0.0494,"tli_or_tmi","Trans-Mars injection","The H3 upper stage put MMX on its Mars transfer; JAXA confirmed separation 71 minutes 13 seconds after liftoff.",{"met_days":67,"type":68,"label":69,"description":70},30,"tcm","TCM-1","First post-TMI cleanup burn.",{"met_days":72,"type":68,"label":73,"description":74},150,"TCM-2","Mid-cruise refinement.",{"met_days":76,"type":68,"label":77,"description":74},250,"TCM-3",{"met_days":79,"type":68,"label":80,"description":81},275,"TCM-4 — final pre-MOI","Final approach targeting.",{"met_days":12,"type":83,"label":84,"description":85},"arrival","Mars orbit insertion","Planned August 2027. Captures into Mars orbit, then moves to a quasi-satellite orbit around Phobos about a month later — a Mars-relative path that keeps MMX close to Phobos without orbiting it directly (Phobos's gravity is too weak for a stable orbit).",{"met_days":87,"type":88,"label":89,"description":90},286.01,"edl_or_oi","Phobos sample collection","Planned 2030, after the Idefix rover lands in 2029. MMX will descend to Phobos's surface, collect ≥10 g of regolith with its coring and pneumatic samplers, and lift off again. The samples return to Earth in 2031 — the first material from a moon of Mars, expected to help settle whether Phobos is a captured asteroid or debris from a giant impact on Mars.",{"source_tier":92,"reference_frame":93,"departure_body":94,"arrival_body":95,"transfer_type":96,"tcm_count":43,"waypoints_helio_au":97},"tier_1_5_hybrid","heliocentric_ecliptic_J2000","earth","mars","type1_lambert",[98,101,